// lexdb's PostgreSQL door: the SQL standard's assertions (ISO/IEC 9075-2, 11.47 <assertion definition>; design/lexdb-new-features.md
// O23 and section 14). PostgreSQL has none, so these answers are lexdb's own, reviewed against the standard and PostgreSQL's
// conventions for constraints (its constraint characteristics, its error states): CREATE ASSERTION [IF NOT EXISTS] name CHECK
// (NOT EXISTS (q) | EXISTS (q) | ALL (q) SATISFY (c)) [[NOT] DEFERRABLE] [INITIALLY IMMEDIATE | DEFERRED], DROP ASSERTION [IF
// EXISTS], SET CONSTRAINTS name | ALL DEFERRED | IMMEDIATE; 23514 `assertion "a" is violated` at the statement, at COMMIT for a
// deferred one (the transaction rolled back); the refused shapes (0A000), 42601, 42710 / 42P07, 42704, 42809, 2BP01 for DROP TABLE
// and DROP COLUMN (CASCADE dropping it), 0A000 for a rename of what it reads; information_schema.ASSERTIONS, CHECK_CONSTRAINTS and
// CONSTRAINT_COLUMN_USAGE (no pg_constraint row). Every write path: INSERT (VALUES, SELECT, ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE / NOTHING),
// UPDATE, DELETE, MERGE, a procedure's statements, the foreign keys' ON DELETE CASCADE / SET NULL / SET DEFAULT and ON UPDATE
// CASCADE / SET DEFAULT, TRUNCATE (checked here; Oracle's door does not), ALTER TABLE ... TYPE (its values cast: the whole query),
// COPY FROM STDIN (text, CSV; ON_ERROR ignore and REJECT_LIMIT, the rows skipped not counted; 1,000 rows: the whole query), and
// UPDATE / DELETE / COPY with batch_rows (each batch checked before it commits: the batches before a failing one stay). Each line
// runs in a transaction rolled back after it (each statement under a savepoint: a failing one undone alone; COMMIT begins the next),
// or, marked "!", statement by statement on their own; once with set lexdb_batch = off and once on: "same" and the answer, or
// DIFFERENT and both.
// timeout: 120
// expect: setup: 36 statements
